1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hiranyakashipu (or Hiranyakasipu) (Sanskrit: हिरण्‍यकशिपु) [golden-haired] is an Asura from the Puranic scriptures of Hinduism. The Puranas describe Hiranyakashipu as an Asura. His younger brother, Hiranyaksha was 
2 CONC slain by Varaha, one of the Avatars of Vishnu and angered by this, Hiranyakashipu decided to gain magical powers by performing a penance for Lord Brahma. He was subsequently killed by the Narasimha Avatara of Lord Vishnu. His tale
2 CONC  depicts the futility of desiring power over others and the strength of God's protection over his fully surrendered devotees (in the case of his son Prahlada).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiranyakashipu
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hiranyakashipu
2 CONT
2 CONT Narasimha slays Hiranyakashipu, as Prahlada watches Hiranyakashipu (or Hiranyakasipu) (Sanskrit:हिरण्‍यकशिपु) [golden-haired] is an Asura from the Puranicscriptures of Hinduism. The Puranas describe Hiranyakashipu as an Asura. His
2 CONC  younger brother,Hiranyaksha was slain by Varaha, one of the Avatars ofVishnu and angered by this, Hiranyakashipu decided to gain magical powers by performing a penance for LordBrahma. He was subsequently killed by the NarasimhaAv
2 CONC atara of Lord Vishnu. His tale depicts the futility of desiring power over others and the strength of God's protection over his fully surrendered devotees (in the case of his son Prahlada). The story of Hiranyakashipu happens in t
2 CONC hree parts. The first has to do with the curse of the Four Kumaras on the gatekeepers of Vaikuntha, Jaya and Vijaya, which causes them to be born as the asuras Hiranyaksha and Hiranyakashipu. The second part deals with Hiranyakash
2 CONC ipu's penance to propitiate Brahma and gain a boon from him. The final part deals with his efforts to kill his son Prahlada (a devotee of Vishnu) and his subsequent death at the hands ofNarasimha. •	 Birth
2 CONT
2 CONT •	
2 CONT
2 CONT •	 •	 According to a story from Bhagavata Purana, Hiranyakashipu and Hiranyaksha are Vishnu's gatekeepers Jaya and Vijaya, born on earth as the result of a curse from the Four Kumaras.According to Sources the birth place of Hirany
2 CONC akashipu is Moolsthan(Present Day Multan). In Satya Yuga, Hiranyakashipu and Hiranyaksha - together called the Hiranyas - were born to Diti (daughter of Daksha Prajapathi) and sage Kashyapa. It is said that asuras were born to the
2 CONC m as a result of their union at the time of dusk, which was said to be an inauspicious time for such a deed.[1] Penance After his brother Hiranyaksha's death at the hands of the Varaha avatar of Vishnu, Hiranyakashipu, started to 
2 CONC abhor Vishnu. To which end he decides to attempt to kill Vishnu by gaining mystical powers, which he believes Brahma, the chief among the devas will award to him if he undergoes many years of great austerity and penance just as Br
2 CONC ahma has awarded to other Rakshasas. This initially seems to work as planned with Brahma becoming pleased by Hiranyakashipu's austerities.[2] Brahma thus appears before Hiranyakashipu and offers him a boon of his choice. Upon Hira
2 CONC nyakashipu's asking for immortality however, Brahma refuses. Hiranyakashipu then requests the following: O my lord, O best of the givers of benediction, if you will kindly grant me the benediction I desire, please let me not meet 
2 CONC death from any of the living entities created by you. Grant me that I not die within any residence or outside any residence, during the daytime or at night, nor on the ground or in the sky. Grant me that my death not be brought by
2 CONC  any being other than those created by you, nor by any weapon, nor by any human being or animal. Grant me that I not meet death from any entity, living or nonliving. Grant me, further, that I not be killed by any demigod or demon 
2 CONC or by any great snake from the lower planets. Since no one can kill you in the battlefield, you have no competitor. Therefore, grant me the benediction that I too may have no rival. Give me sole lordship over all the living entiti
2 CONC es and presiding deities, and give me all the glories obtained by that position. Furthermore, give me all the mystic powers attained by long austerities and the practice of yoga, for these cannot be lost at any time.[3] Prahlada a
2 CONC nd Narasimha
2 CONT
2 CONT Narasimha kills Hiranyakashipu, as Prahlada and his mother bow before Lord Narasimha Whilst Hiranyakashipu had been performing the penance to be granted this boon, his home had been attacked by Indra and the other devas,[4] seizin
2 CONC g the opportunity in his absence. At this point the divine sage,Narada intervened in order to protect Hiranyakashipu's wife, Kayadhu,[5] who he describes as 'sinless'.[6] Following this event Narada takes Kayadhu into his care and
2 CONC  while under the guidance of Narada, her unborn child (Hiranyakashipu's son)Prahlada, became affected by the transcendental instructions of the sage even at such a young stage of development. Thus, Prahlada when later growing as a
2 CONC  child began to show symptoms of this earlier training by Narada, gradually becoming recognised as a devoted follower of Vishnu, much to his father's disappointment.[7] Hiranyakashipu eventually becomes so angry and upset at his s
2 CONC on's devotion to Vishnu (who he sees as his mortal enemy) that he decides he must kill him[8] but each time he to attempts to kill the boy, Prahlada is protected by Vishnu's mystical power. When asked, Prahlada refuses to acknowle
2 CONC dge his father as the supreme lord of the universe and claims that Vishnu is all-pervading and omnipresent. To which Hiranyakashipu points to a nearby pillar and asks if 'his Vishnu' is in it: "O most unfortunate Prahlada, you hav
2 CONC e always described a supreme being other than me, a supreme being who is above everything, who is the controller of everyone, and who is all-pervading. But where is He? If He is everywhere, then why is He not present before me in 
2 CONC this pillar?"[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Vishnu as Narasimha kills Hiranyakashipu, stone sculpture from Halebidu, Karnataka Prahlada then answers, He was, He is and He will be. In an alternate version of the story, Prahlada answers He is in pillars, and he is in the leas
2 CONC t twig. Hiranyakashipu, unable to control his anger, smashes the pillar with his mace, and then following a tumultuous sound, Vishnu in the form ofNarasimha appears from it and in defence of Prahlada moves to attack his father. In
2 CONC  order to kill Hiranyakashipu and not upset the boon given by Brahma, the form of Narasimha was chosen. Hiranyakashipu could not be killed by human, deva or animal, Narasimha is neither one of these, as he is a form of Vishnu inca
2 CONC rnate as a part-human, part-animal. He comes upon Hiranyakashipu at twilight (when it is neither day nor night) on the threshold of a courtyard (neither indoors nor out), and puts the demon on his lap (neither earth nor space). Us
2 CONC ing his nails (neither animate nor inanimate) as weapons, he disembowels and kills the demon.[10] Even after killing Hiranyakashipu none of the present demigods were able to calm Narasimha's fury, not evenShiva. So all the gods an
2 CONC d goddesses called His consort, the goddess Lakshmi, but she was also unable to do so. Then, at the request of Brahma, Prahlada was presented to Narasimha, and finally, he was calmed by the prayers of his devotee.[11] Holi See als
2 CONC o: Holika and Holi One of Hiranyakashipu's attempts to kill Prahlada was to have him sit on a burning pyre with his sisterHolika. Holika had a special gift that prevented her from being harmed by fire. Prahlada chanted Vishnu's na
2 CONC me and in the battle of good against evil, Holika was burnt down but nothing happened to Prahlad. The burning of Holika is celebrated as Holi festival in Hinduism.[citation needed]
