1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Parvati (IAST: Pārvatī) is the Hindu goddess of fertility, love and devotion; as well as of divine strength and power.[3][4][5] She is the gentle and nurturing aspect of the Hindu goddess Shakti and one of the cent
2 CONC ral deities of the Goddess-oriented Shakta sect. She is the mother goddess in Hinduism,[1][6] and has many attributes and aspects. Each of her aspects is expressed with a different name, giving her over 100 names in regional Hindu
2 CONC  stories of India.[7] Along with Lakshmi (goddess of wealth and prosperity) and Saraswati (goddess of knowledge and learning), she forms the trinity of Hindu goddesses (Tridevi).[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Parvati is the wife of the Hindu god Shiva - the protector and regenerator of universe and all life.[9] She is the daughter of the mountain king Himavan and mother Mena.[10] Parvati is the mother of Hindu deities Ganesha and Karti
2 CONC keya. Some communities also believe her to be the sister of the god Vishnu and the river-goddess Ganga.[11][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parvati
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The one who rules this world. Everything in this world is contained in HER. All forms in the world are HER forms. All living things are part of HER.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is Devi Adi Parashakthi who performs the tasks of the Tri-Murthys : Siva, Vishnu ad Brahma. She is the 8 Siddhis, 7 Rishis, Lords of the 8 directions, 11 Rudras and many more.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is not only worshipped by men but also by the devas. She is present in everything we see. She is represented in plants, the animals, the birds, the sea, the sky and the holy river.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is Sakthi ( Power ) who drives this world. She is the mother of the mankind.
2 CONT
2 CONT Devi Features
2 CONT
2 CONT The most important features of Devi Parashakti mythology and theology is that she have two forms, namely
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. The Auspicious form
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. The Inauspicious form
2 CONT
2 CONT Auspicious Form
2 CONT
2 CONT As auspicious form, she manifests as Lakshmi, Parvati, Sati and Prthivi. She plays the positive roles of protection, fertility and establishment of dharmic order, cultural creativity, wifely duty and material abundance.
2 CONT
2 CONT These encompases three aspects
2 CONT
2 CONT a. as the granter of wisdom, learning and liberation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her names appear many times in the following text :
2 CONT
2 CONT Aryastave text she is called : Mukti ( liberation ), She who speaks the language of Brahman, She who is the knowledge of Brahman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mahabharata : Liberator and knowledge of Brahman as well as the mother of the vedas, Intelligence and the destroyer of ignorance and mankind fetters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lalitha-Sahasranama : Mahabuddhi (The great intelligence ), Vijnanabhanarupini ( She whose form is a mass of knowledge ), Prajnatnika ( She who is wisdom itself ), Pasupasavimocini ( She who release creatures from bondage )
2 CONT
2 CONT b. as embodiment of female beauty and the exciter of desire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many Text describe Devi extraordinar beauty
2 CONT
2 CONT saundaryalahari : one of the famous hymn praising the Devi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lalitha-Sahasranama : Ramya (the beautiful one), Kanta ( loveliness ), Vamanayana ( she who have beautiful eyes ), Ratirupa ( she whose form is Rati )
2 CONT
2 CONT c. as the source of food and nourishment
2 CONT
2 CONT She is often associated with earth itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Shakti Sadhana
2 CONT
2 CONT Lalitha-Sahasranama : Annanda ( she who gives food ), Pusti (she who gives nourishment ), Satakasi ( she who has on hundred eyes ), Sakambhari ( she who bestows vegetables ), Annapurna ( she who is full of food ), Jagaddhatri ( sh
2 CONC e who supports the world or another )
2 CONT
2 CONT Inauspicious Form
2 CONT
2 CONT The Devi is said to possess fierce and terrible appearance. Often these forms are associated with war, blood, destruction, death, hunger and anger.
2 CONT
2 CONT These terrible forms arises in two context, namely
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. To maintain cosmic order
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Asserting her power as that greater than Siva
2 CONT
2 CONT TO maintain cosmic order
2 CONT
2 CONT Devi often takes in fierce form when she enters into battle. As guardian of the Cosmos, she appears in a form of a great warrior, battling against Demons on behalf of the gods. Nevertheless in most text it is said that the Devi on
2 CONC ly take the fierce form when she has actually entered the battle or when she is being provoked by the insults of her enemies. The Devi transforms herself, and then multiplies into ferocious goddess or goddesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Devi terrible forms often betray the benigh qualities of the goddess, and it threatens the world. This action suggest the dangerous and uncontrolled facets of the goddess when she loses control.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is described as rending her enemies limb from limb, tearng their flesh with her teeth and drinking their blood, when then intoxicates her. Thirsting for more blood or dancing drunkenly out of control, she herself becomes a thr
2 CONC eat to the cosmos and must be tamed or subdued. She can becomes dangerous to her own allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Asserting her power as the greater than Siva
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mahavidyas ( the great manfestation ) played a central role in Sati's attempt to overpower or frighten Siva, so that she may have her own way. Even though Sati played the submissive wife, she contains fearsome and independent 
2 CONC aspects that easily overwhelmed Siva.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other aspects of Devi
2 CONT
2 CONT Devi is often describe in three concepts namely : SHAKTI, MAYA and PRAKRTI
