1 TITL Rishi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Parashara (IAST: Parāśara) was a maharishi and the author of many ancient Indian texts. He is accredited as the author of the first Purana, the Vishnu Purana, before his son Vyasa wrote it in its present form. He w
2 CONC as the grandson of Vasishtha, the son of Śakti Maharṣi, and the father of Vyasa. There are several texts which give reference to Parashara as an author/speaker. Modern scholars believe that there were many individuals who used thi
2 CONC s name throughout time whereas others assert that the same Parashara taught these various texts and the time of writing them varied. The actual sage himself never wrote the texts, he was known as a traveling teacher, and the vario
2 CONC us texts attributed to him are given in reference to Parashara being the speaker to his student[citation needed]. He is the third member of the Ṛṣi Paramparā of the Advaita Guru Paramparā. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogy[edit] 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Vedas, Brahma created Vasishtha, who, with Arundhati, had a son named Śakti Maharṣi who sired Parashara. With Satyavati, Parashara fathered Vyasa. Vyāsa sired Dhritarashtra, Pandu and Vidura through his deceased b
2 CONC rother's wives. Vyāsa also sired Shuka through his wife, Jābāli's daughter Pinjalā. Thus Parashara was the great-grandfather of both the warring parties of the Mahābhārata, the Kauravas and the Pandavas. Parashara is used as a got
2 CONC ra for the ancestors and their off springs thereon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Life[edit] Parashara was raised by his grandfather Vasishtha because he lost his father at an early age. His father, Śakti Muni, was on a journey and came across an angry Rakshasa (demon) who had once been a king but was turned in
2 CONC to a demon feeding on human flesh as a curse from Viśvamitra. The demon devoured Parashara’s father. In the Viṣṇu Purāṇa, Parashara speaks about his anger from this:[1] "I had heard that my father had been devoured by a Rākṣasa em
2 CONC ployed by Viśvamitra: violent anger seized me, and I commenced a sacrifice for the destruction of the Rākṣasas: hundreds of them were reduced to ashes by the rite, when, as they were about to be entirely exterminated, my grandfath
2 CONC er Vasishtha said to me: Enough, my child; let thy wrath be appeased: the Rākṣasas are not culpable: thy father's death was the work of destiny. Anger is the passion of fools; it becometh not a wise man. By whom, it may be asked, 
2 CONC is anyone killed? Every man reaps the consequences of his own acts. Anger, my son, is the destruction of all that man obtains by arduous exertions, of fame, and of devout austerities; and prevents the attainment of heaven or of em
2 CONC ancipation. The chief sages always shun wrath: be not subject to its influence, my child. Let no more of these unoffending spirits of darkness be consumed. Mercy is the might of the righteous.”[citation needed] Parashara Muni (Sag
2 CONC e), at the wish of Lord Vishnu, Brahma and Mahadev, who maintain, create and destroy in time the entire universe, on one of his travels across the country, halted for the night in a little hamlet on the banks of the river Yamuna. 
2 CONC He was put up in the house of the fisherman-chieftain Dusharaj. When dawn broke, the chief asked his daughter, Matsyagandha, whose name means "one with the smell of fish", to ferry the sage to his next destination. When in the fer
2 CONC ry, Parashara was attracted by the beautiful girl. He created an island within the river by his mystic potency and asked her to land the boat there. Seeing people on the river's bank, she demurred, at which time the sage created a
2 CONC  dense fog which enveloped the entire river. Parashara blessed her with a son, Krishna Dvaipāyana, who was dark-complexioned and hence may be called by the name Krishna (black), and also the name Dwaipayana, meaning 'island-born'.
2 CONC  He later compiled the classic Vedic literatures of India, and so is called Vyasa who is the 17th incarnations of Lord Vishnu. Parashara granted her the boon that the finest fragrance may emit from her person. She was thereafter k
2 CONC nown as Satyavati (pure fragrance). Leaving Satyavati in the care of Vyasa, Parashara proceeded to perform Tapas (intense meditation). Later Vysasa also became a Rishi and Satyavati returned to her father's house, and in due cours
2 CONC e, married Śantanu. Parashara was known as the "limping sage". He had his leg wounded during an attack on his āśrama. When a ṛṣi dies he merges back into an element or an archetype. When Sage Parashara was walking through a dense 
2 CONC forest he and his students were attacked by wolves. He was unable to get away in his old age with a lame leg he left this world merging into the wolves.[2] The birthplace of Parashara Muni is believed to be at Panhala fort in Kolh
2 CONC apur district of Maharashtra. A cave supposed to be of Parāśāra Muni is present at the fort. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ṛgveda[edit] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Ṛgveda, Parashara, son of Śakti Muni (Parashara Śāktya), is the seer of verses 1.65-73 which are all in praise of Agni (the sacred fire), and part of 9.97 (v.31-44) which is in praise of Soma. Below is 1.73.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT devo na yaḥ savitā satyamanmā kratvā nipāti vṛjanāni viṣvā purupraṣasto amatirna satya ātmeva Sevo didhiṣāyyo bhūt 
2 CONT
2 CONT He who is like the divine Sun, who knows the truth (of all things), preserves by his actions (his votaries) in all encounters; like nature, he is unchangeable and, like soul, is the source of all happiness: he is ever to be cheris
2 CONC hed.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parashara 
