1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sumitra (Sanskrit: सुमित्रा, Tamil: Cumittirai, Burmese: Thumitra, Malay: Samutra, Khmer: Sramut) in the Indian Ramayana epic, was the third[1] of King Dasaratha's three wives and a queen of Ayodhyā. She was the mo
2 CONC ther of twins Lakshmana and Shatrughna. She came from the ancient kingdom of Kashi. She was supposed to be the wisest of the all the wives of Dasaratha. She was the one to first realize that Rama was the incarnation of Lord Naraya
2 CONC na. She even encouraged Lakshmana to accompany Rama and serve him during his exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a popular story about why twins were born to Sumitra. Raja(King) Dasaratha of Ayodhya was not having any children despite of three queens. None of the queens was able to give heir to Ikshvaku dynasty. Raja Dasaratha start
2 CONC ed to remain sad by thinking that after him his dynasty will come to an end. He was very eager to have a son who can take care on Ayodhya’s throne and continue Eshwaku dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guru Vasistha who was raj purohit of Ayodhya kingdom asked the reason behind king’s sadness. The king told the reason of not having a son and heir of dynasty. On Guru Vasistha's request, king Dasharatha did a Yagya (a type of wors
2 CONC hip in Hinduism). From this Yagya he got two plates of sweets which he gave to his queens. The king gave one plate of sweet to Kausalya and other plate to Kaikeyi and asked them to share their plates with the third queen Sumitra. 
2 CONC Sumitra got one bite from Kausalya’s plate and one bite from Kaikeyi’s plate that’s why she had twins. And Kausalya and Kaikeyi got one son each. Sumitra’s sons were later named by guru Vasistha as Lakshmana and Shatrughana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumitra 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sumitra (Sanskrit: सुमित्रा, Tamil: சுமித்திரை, Burmese: Thumitra, Malay: Samutra, Khmer: Sramut) in the Indian Ramayana epic, was the third[1] of King Dasaratha's three wives and a queen of Ayodhyā. She was the mother of twins La
2 CONC kshmana and Shatrughna. She came from the ancient kingdom of Kashi. She was supposed to be the wisest of the wives of Dasaratha. She was the one to first realize that Rama was the incarnation of Lord Narayana. She even encouraged 
2 CONC Lakshmana to accompany Rama and serve him during his exile. There is a popular story about why twins were born to Sumitra. Dasaratha, despite having three wives had no children and none of the queens had been able to give him an h
2 CONC eir to the Ikshvaku dynasty. The thought of his dynasty ending with him saddened Dasaratha and he was very eager to have a son who would take care of the throne of Ayodhya. Guru Vasistha who was the raj (royal) purohit of Ayodhya 
2 CONC asked the King the reason for his sadness and the King conveyed his worries about the lack of a male heir to his dynasty. On Vasistha's request, Dasharatha did a Yagya at the end of which he gave a plate of sweets each to Kausalya
2 CONC  and Kaikeyi and asked them to share their plates with the third queen Sumitra. Sumitra got one bite from Kausalya’s plate and one from Kaikeyi’s and thus gave birth to twins. With Kausalya and Kaikeyi each bearing one son. Sumitr
2 CONC a’s sons were later named by guru Vasistha as Lakshmana and Shatrughana.
2 CONT
2 CONT SUMITRA ON RAMA
2 CONT
2 CONT Sumithra is not appearing in many scenes in Ramayanam. There are only two scenes in which she figures. In both these two scenes, she depicts what extraordinary qualities she possessed, to reveal her magnanimity, spirit of detachme
2 CONC nt, wisdom and more importantly her ability to recognize in Rama, his divinity.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this passage, Sumithra reveals her extraordinary qualities of showing remarkable magnanimity, forbearance, detachment, and to render the wise counsel to the right man at the right time. She also strongly establishes that she ha
2 CONC d BRAHMAGNANAM, that is awareness that Rama was Lord Narayana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Do not give scope for misgivings to haunt your heart. You are the one to console others. For such a woman like you, Rama is the son. There is no one in the world who is superior to Rama, who knows and treads on sacred path”
2 CONT
2 CONT Sumithra thus refers to Rama as Lord Narayana Himself. Her words are so self revealing that any commentary is hardly needed. It is remarkable that Rama’s step-mother Sumithra saw in him all the features of God but neither his own 
2 CONC mother Kausalya nor his father Dasaratha had this realisation!
